-# Backend rebuild — Thymeleaf → REST API
-
-Full rewrite to match `CONTRACT.md` from the frontend phase. Every endpoint
-in that contract is implemented. This doc covers what changed and why,
-what I could and couldn't verify, and what you need to do before running it.
-
-## ⚠️ Read this first: I could not compile this
-
-This sandbox has no `javac`/Maven/internet access to Maven Central — I
-could not run `mvn compile` or anything that touches real dependency jars
-(Spring, JJWT, Hibernate). What I *did* do to compensate:
-
-- Manually cross-referenced every repository method call, entity
-  getter/setter, and import against the actual source files in your repo
-  (not from memory — I re-cloned and re-read the real files throughout).
-- Checked brace/parenthesis balance and import correctness across every
-  file with a script.
-- Pulled the new price-parsing regex out and verified it standalone
-  against 11 real cases (all your existing price string formats) using
-  Python's equivalent regex engine, since I had no JVM to test the real
-  Java version.
-- Kept the JWT code to the documented `jjwt 0.12.x` fluent API exactly as
-  published, and removed an `@NonNull` annotation I'd added that wasn't
-  load-bearing, specifically to avoid risking a package that may have
-  moved in this very new Spring Boot version (4.1.0).
-
-**Please run `mvn clean compile` (or just open it in your IDE) as the very
-first step** before anything else. If something doesn't compile, it's
-most likely a tiny thing (an import path that shifted in this Spring Boot
-version) — tell me the error and I'll fix it immediately.
-
-## What changed, file by file
-
-### Removed (fully replaced, not needed anymore)
-- `controller/Index.java`, `controller/BookingController.java` (old),
-  `controller/AdminController.java` (old) — Thymeleaf page controllers
-- `security/AdminSecurity.java` — session/form-login security config
-- `config/TenantContext.java` — the per-process `app.tenant.id` resolver
-- `src/main/resources/templates/**` and `src/main/resources/static/**` —
-  provably orphaned the moment the controllers returning those view names
-  were gone. Your real frontend lives outside this repo now.
-
-### Entities changed
-- **`Company`** — added `domain` (unique). This is what `PublicTenantResolver`
-  matches against the `Origin` header.
-- **`AdminUser`** — new. `email` (unique, login identifier), `passwordHash`
-  (BCrypt), `company` (FK). Replaces the old single hardcoded
-  `InMemoryUserDetailsManager` admin entirely.
-- **`BookingRequest`** — `service` (free-text code) → `serviceOffering`
-  (real FK, nullable). This is the fix for the old revenue-estimation hack:
-  bookings now reference a real `ServiceOffering`, so the dashboard can read
-  its *actual* price instead of guessing from a lookup table.
-
-`AddOn.java` / `PricingPlan.java` were already dead, unused, non-`@Entity`
-leftover files before I touched anything — left alone, harmless.
-
-### New: full service/controller/security layers
-- **Public** (`CompanyController`, `ServiceCatalogController`, `BookingController`):
-  resolve tenant via `PublicTenantResolver` (Origin header → `Company.domain`).
-- **Admin** (`AuthController`, `AdminDashboardController`, `AdminBookingController`,
-  `AdminServiceController`, `AdminAccountController`): resolve tenant via
-  `CurrentAdmin.companyId()`, which reads the JWT-derived security context —
-  never from anything the client sends.
-- **`JwtService`** / **`JwtAuthFilter`** / **`SecurityConfig`**: stateless JWT
-  auth, CORS enabled (wildcard origin, no credentials — see code comments for
-  why that's safe here), CSRF disabled (no cookies exist to forge against).
-- **`DashboardAnalyticsService`**: same proven logic from the Thymeleaf phase,
-  now computing real revenue via a `$NN` regex against the linked
-  `ServiceOffering.price` instead of the old guessed price map.
-- **`ServiceCatalogService`**: public reads now *persist* a starter catalog
-  the first time any category (services/plans/addons) is empty for a
-  business — so a booking's `serviceOfferingId` always has a real row to
-  point at, even for a business that hasn't customized anything yet. If an
-  owner later deletes everything in a category, the next visitor re-seeds
-  it the same way (a deliberate tradeoff — see the code comment if you ever
-  want a business to be able to keep a category permanently empty).
-
-### `pom.xml`
-Added `jjwt-api`, `jjwt-impl`, `jjwt-jackson` (0.12.6). Nothing removed —
-the now-unused `thymeleaf` / `thymeleaf-test` / `thymeleaf-extras-springsecurity6`
-dependencies are still there (harmless dead weight); I left them rather than
-risk removing something without being able to recompile. Delete them
-yourself whenever you want a fully clean dependency tree.
-
-### `application.properties`
-- Removed `app.tenant.id`, `app.admin.username`, `app.admin.password` —
-  meaningless now that one instance serves every business.
-- Added `app.jwt.secret` (required, no default — set `JWT_SECRET` env var,
-  ≥32 chars) and `app.jwt.expiration-ms` (optional, defaults to 24h).
-- Lowered `hikari.maximum-pool-size` 10 → 3, per what we discussed about
-  one shared instance serving multiple tenants — adjust up only if you
-  actually see pool exhaustion under real load.
-- `ddl-auto=create-drop` is **still there, unchanged** — still fine for now
-  with no real client data, still the thing to fix (→ `validate` + real
-  migrations) before this matters in production. Flagging it again since
-  the schema itself just changed meaningfully.
-
-### New files
-- **`.env.example`** (`src/main/resources/`) — matches your existing
-  gitignored `.env` convention, documents every required env var.
-- **`onboarding-template.sql`** (repo root) — matches your existing
-  gitignored `onboarding-template.sql` convention. Two `INSERT`s (company +
-  admin user) with a **real, working BCrypt hash** for a temporary password
-  (`ChangeMe123!`) — not a placeholder, I actually generated it. New client
-  logs in with that, changes it immediately via the change-password endpoint.
-- **`config/DataInitializer.java`** — seeds one demo company + admin login
-  on startup, but *only* if the `company` table is completely empty, so it
-  can never touch a database that already has real data. Gives you a
-  working local setup with zero manual SQL.
-
-## Running this locally
-
-1. Set env vars (see `.env.example`): `DATABASE_URL`, `DATABASE_USERNAME`,
-   `DATABASE_PASSWORD`, `JWT_SECRET` (`openssl rand -base64 48`).
-2. Run the app. First boot seeds the demo company + admin (see console
-   output for the exact credentials) — only happens once, only on an
-   empty database.
-3. **Serve the storefront over a real local HTTP server, not by opening
-   the HTML file directly.** `file://` origins send `Origin: null`, which
-   will never match a real domain. The seeded demo company's domain is
-   `localhost:5500` — either serve the storefront on that exact port
-   (`python3 -m http.server 5500` from inside `storefront/`), or change
-   `DEV_DOMAIN` in `DataInitializer.java` to match whatever port you
-   actually use.
-4. Admin panel: log in with the seeded email/password from the console output.
-
-## Known gaps / things I deliberately didn't build
-
-- **No endpoint to edit company profile info** (name, city, phone, stats)
-  after onboarding — still a direct-DB edit via the onboarding template.
-  Neither the old Thymeleaf admin panel nor the new fetch-based one ever had
-  a "company settings" screen, so I didn't invent a backend endpoint with no
-  frontend to call it. Easy to add (a `PUT /admin/company` + a settings page)
-  if you want it — say the word.
-- **Price parsing assumes a `$NN` substring somewhere in the price text.**
-  An admin entering "Call for a quote" with no dollar sign contributes $0 to
-  revenue calculations rather than erroring — silent-but-safe, worth knowing.
-- **CORS is wide open (`*`)** by design, explained in `SecurityConfig`'s
-  comments — revisit only if you start relying on cookies anywhere, which
-  nothing here currently does.
